<h3 id="pay-yourself-to-form-a-writing-habit">Pay yourself to form a writing habit</h3>
<p>I write words every day. But plenty of days go by where I don’t <em>write</em> write. I’d really like to turn that around.</p>
<p>Here are some ideas that might help. All of them pay you to form the habit, and one even uses regular currency to do so! The others pay you by adding incremental strength and agility to your writing muscles.</p>
<p><strong>Morning Pages</strong> - Set a timer and write whatever comes to mind until you hear the beep. Attach this habit to whatever you normally <em>want</em> to do first thing in the morning, but do this one first. Timer recommendations: TimeTimer and Liquid Timer.</p>
<p><strong>Koala Quill</strong> - Get paid a dollar to write for 15 minutes every day.<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>Stop abandoning your drafts. Get paid just for showing up.” It’s an interesting concept! In a nutshell: you put money into the pot, and get it back bit by bit as you remember to write. They’ve made it mighty hard (if not impossible) to game the system.</p>
<p>If adrenalin motivates you more than money, try <strong><a href="https://www.squibler.io/dangerous-writing-prompt-app" target="_blank">Squibler’s Dangerous Writing App</a></strong>.<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>Don’t stop typing or all progress will be lost!” You can set a time limit or a word count goal. If that’s not enough pressure for you, there’s also hardcore mode where your writing is hidden as you type, and backspace is disabled. Access to everything you wrote is restored once you reach your goal. Similar: <a href="https://thewritepractice.com/write-or/" target="_blank">Write Or…</a>.</p>
<hr>
<h3 id="sorry-vision-impaired-friends">Sorry, vision-impaired friends!</h3>
<p>I’ve just realised that visuals made from text might look nice to sighted people, but they’re a right pain in the ears for the vision impaired. Colin listened to the built-in reader version of <a href="https://ellanew.com/2026/06/29/ptpl-214-text-progress-bars-dfos-discussion-zone" class="wikilink">last week’s post</a> on <a href="https://miscellaneplans.medium.com/ptpl-214-hand-typed-progress-bars-plus-a-new-plain-text-discussion-zone-70b3756988dd" target="_blank">Medium</a> and got to hear a lot of<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>zero zero zero zero zero zero dash dash dash”. Fun for the first 2 seconds, I am sure.</p>
<p>No doubt a full x/y axis table would have been completely nonsensical audio torture.</p>
<p>In future I’ll try to remember to put that sort of thing at the end of the post to make it easier to skip, or, at the very least, give fair warning at the start. Should I forget, please do a) be patient, and b) remind me nicely so I can take care of it!</p>
<p><span class="small-caps">FYI</span>, using a screen reader isn’t exclusively for the vision impaired. You might also find it handy to catch up on articles while doing other things with your eyes.</p>
<hr>
<p>Transcript of the handwriting in the cover picture:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Morning pages don’t need to be read by anyone other than you, and even then, it’s not obligatory to review your own work. Although you might regret it if you don’t as this kind of writing — when it’s really in the flow — births more gems than sentences crafted carefully in your head before they ever make it to paper. Remember to write in pencil! There’s a fluidity here, and an implied impermanence that gives subtle permission to your psyche to open the gates of creativity — the same gates that seem stuck fast when you’re under a deadline.</p>
</blockquote>
